{"type":"message","id":"f37bbf98","parentId":"46c47717","timestamp":"2026-03-08T22:06:04.009Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[Sun 2026-03-08 23:04 GMT+1] You are an expert AI systems architect.\n\n**Context:**\n- OpenClaw has a Python neuro-memory-agent (8 brain mechanisms: Bayesian surprise, event segmentation, two-stage retrieval, consolidation, forgetting, interference resolution, online learning)\n- Currently NOT active - sitting at /Users/tolga/Desktop/neuro-memory-agent\n- Evolution service exists but uses simple template-based proposals\n- User wants to wire neuro-memory to evolution for smarter proposals\n- **Problem:** Python IPC is slow. Can't compute Bayesian surprise on every event emit()\n\n**Question:** How should we integrate neuro-memory-agent into OpenClaw's evolution system?\n\nConstraints:\n1. Must not slow down hot path (event emit, tool execution)\n2. Should help evolution learn from past failures\n3. Can use batch queue (already implemented - 10 events / 2s flush)\n4. Only 1 evolution run per week typically\n\nGive me:\n- Architecture pattern (where to wire)\n- Performance strategy (async? batch? selective?)\n- Code sketch for integration\n- Trade-offs\n\nBe specific and opinionated. Focus on practical implementation."}],"timestamp":1773007564008}}
{"type":"message","id":"b19db1fc","parentId":"f37bbf98","timestamp":"2026-03-08T22:07:50.517Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a great systems architecture question. Let me think through this carefully.\n\n**Context Summary:**\n1. OpenClaw has a Python neuro-memory-agent with sophisticated brain mechanisms\n2. Currently inactive at /Users/tolga/Desktop/neuro-memory-agent\n3. Evolution service uses simple template-based proposals\n4. Python IPC is slow - can't compute Bayesian surprise on every event emit()\n5. Batch queue exists (10 events / 2s flush)\n6. Evolution runs ~1/week (low frequency)\n\n**Key Insight:** Evolution is NOT on the hot path. It's a background process that runs weekly. This gives us a HUGE design advantage.\n\n**Architecture Options:**\n\n1. **Hot Path Integration** (BAD) - Call Python on every event emit\n   - ❌ Too slow, blocks event processing\n   - ❌ Defeats the purpose of fast event system\n\n2. **Batch Queue Consumer** (BETTER) - Python reads from batch queue\n   - ✅ Non-blocking to hot path\n   - ✅ Already have batch queue (10 events / 2s)\n   - ⚠️ Still need IPC mechanism\n\n3. **Lazy Evaluation / Pull-Based** (BEST) - Neuro-memory computes ONLY when evolution runs\n   - ✅ No IPC overhead on hot path\n   - ✅ Evolution runs weekly - plenty of time to compute\n   - ✅ Can process ALL historical events, not just recent\n   - ✅ Bayesian surprise computed on-demand, not proactively\n\n**My Recommendation: Hybrid Approach**\n\n**Pattern 1: Event Collection (Hot Path)**\n- Keep event collection simple and fast\n- Events flow to batch queue (already implemented)\n- Batch queue flushes to persistent storage (JSONL/SQLite)\n- NO Python calls on hot path\n\n**Pattern 2: Neuro-Memory Processing (Cold Path)**\n- When evolution runs (weekly), it triggers neuro-memory\n\n\n- Neuro-memory reads accumulated event history and applies sophisticated processing\n- Generates insights for evolution service to use in generating proposals\n- This approach ensures minimal overhead during active system use\n\nThe strategy leverages the infrequent evolution runs to perform computationally intensive neural processing without impacting real-time performance. By decoupling event collection from deep analysis, we create a more efficient and flexible system architecture.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"# Neuro-Memory + Evolution Integration Architecture\n\n## TL;DR\n\n**Use lazy evaluation (pull-based)**. Don't compute anything on the hot path. Neuro-memory processes events ONLY when evolution runs (weekly). This avoids all IPC overhead during normal operation.\n\n---\n\n## Architecture Pattern: \"Cold Compute, Hot Store\"\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                        HOT PATH (milliseconds)                  │\n│  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────────┐    ┌────────────────────┐    │\n│  │  Event   │───▶│  Batch Queue │───▶│  Event Store       │    │\n│  │  emit()  │    │  (10/2s)     │    │  (JSONL/SQLite)    │    │\n│  └──────────┘    └──────────────┘    └────────────────────┘    │\n│                         NO PYTHON HERE                          │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                              │\n                              │ writes only\n                              ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                      COLD PATH (seconds-minutes)                │\n│                                                                 │\n│  ┌───────────────────┐                                        │\n│  │  Evolution Run    │ (weekly trigger)                       │\n│  │  (TypeScript)     │                                        │\n│  └────────┬──────────┘                                        │\n│           │ 1. spawn Python process                            │\n│           ▼                                                    │\n│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │\n│  │  Neuro-Memory Agent (Python)                          │     │\n│  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │     │\n│  │  │  1. Load events from Event Store (last N days)  │  │     │\n│  │  │  2. Compute Bayesian surprise on event batches  │  │     │\n│  │  │  3. Event segmentation (detect boundaries)      │  │     │\n│  │  │  4. Two-stage retrieval (find similar failures) │  │     │\n│  │  │  5. Consolidate patterns into memory            │  │     │\n│  │  │  6. Apply forgetting curve                      │  │     │\n│  │  │  7. Resolve interference conflicts              │  │     │\n│  │  │  8. Online learning: update weights             │  │     │\n│  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │     │\n│  │                         │                              │     │\n│  │                         ▼ 9. output JSON               │     │\n│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │\n│           │                                                    │\n│           ▼ stdout (JSON)                                      │\n│  ┌───────────────────┐                                        │\n│  │  Evolution Service│                                        │\n│  │  - Parse insights │                                        │\n│  │  - Generate smarter proposals using:                     │\n│  │    * Failure patterns                                     │\n│  │    * Success patterns                                     │\n│  │    * Surprise correlations                               │\n│  └───────────────────┘                                        │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n---\n\n## Why This Works\n\n### 1. **Zero Hot Path Overhead**\n- Events write to queue/store synchronously (fast, TypeScript)\n- No Python IPC until evolution runs\n- Batch queue already exists - just needs persistence layer\n\n### 2. **Lazy Computation is Fine**\n- Evolution runs weekly → neuro-memory has seconds/minutes to process\n- Bayesian surprise doesn't need to be real-time\n- Can process THOUSANDS of events in one batch (efficient)\n\n### 3. **Full Event History Available**\n- Not limited to \"last 10 events\"\n- Can analyze patterns across days/weeks\n- Better for detecting slow-moving failures\n\n### 4. **Simple Failure Mode**\n- If Python crashes, evolution still runs (just dumber proposals)\n- If neuro-memory is slow, evolution waits (not user-facing)\n- Easy to debug (single process invocation)\n\n---\n\n## Implementation Sketch\n\n### File: `src/services/neuro-memory-bridge.ts`\n\n```typescript\nimport { spawn } from 'child_process';\nimport { readFileSync } from 'fs';\nimport path from 'path';\n\ninterface NeuroMemoryInsights {\n  failurePatterns: Array<{\n    pattern: string;\n    frequency: number;\n    lastSeen: string;\n    similarPastFailures: string[];\n  }>;\n  surprisingEvents: Array<{\n    eventId: string;\n    surpriseScore: number;\n    reason: string;\n  }>;\n  recommendations: Array<{\n    type: 'avoid' | 'prefer' | 'investigate';\n    context: string;\n    confidence: number;\n  }>;\n  memoryConsolidation: {\n    newPatterns: number;\n    forgottenPatterns: number;\n    reinforcedPatterns: number;\n  };\n}\n\nexport class NeuroMemoryBridge {\n  private pythonPath = '/Users/tolga/Desktop/neuro-memory-agent';\n  private eventStorePath: string;\n\n  constructor(eventStorePath: string) {\n    this.eventStorePath = eventStorePath;\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Called by evolution service when it's time to generate proposals.\n   * Spawns Python process, waits for insights.\n   * Timeout: 60 seconds (plenty for weekly run)\n   */\n  async computeInsights(\n    options: {\n      daysLookback?: number;\n      focusArea?: 'failures' | 'all' | 'surprises';\n    } = {}\n  ): Promise<NeuroMemoryInsights | null> {\n    const daysLookback = options.daysLookback || 30;\n    const focusArea = options.focusArea || 'failures';\n\n    return new Promise((resolve) => {\n      const args = [\n        path.join(this.pythonPath, 'analyze_for_evolution.py'),\n        '--event-store', this.eventStorePath,\n        '--days', String(daysLookback),\n        '--focus', focusArea,\n        '--output', 'json'\n      ];\n\n      const python = spawn('python3', args, {\n        cwd: this.pythonPath,\n        timeout: 60000 // 60s timeout\n      });\n\n      let stdout = '';\n      let stderr = '';\n\n      python.stdout.on('data', (data) => {\n        stdout += data.toString();\n      });\n\n      python.stderr.on('data', (data) => {\n        stderr += data.toString();\n      });\n\n      python.on('close', (code) => {\n        if (code !== 0) {\n          console.error('[NeuroMemory] Python exited with code', code);\n          console.error('[NeuroMemory] stderr:', stderr);\n          resolve(null); // Graceful degradation\n          return;\n        }\n\n        try {\n          const insights = JSON.parse(stdout) as NeuroMemoryInsights;\n          console.log('[NeuroMemory] Computed insights:', {\n            failurePatterns: insights.failurePatterns.length,\n            surprises: insights.surprisingEvents.length,\n            recommendations: insights.recommendations.length\n          });\n          resolve(insights);\n        } catch (err) {\n          console.error('[NeuroMemory] Failed to parse output:', err);\n          resolve(null);\n        }\n      });\n\n      python.on('error', (err) => {\n        console.error('[NeuroMemory] Failed to spawn Python:', err);\n        resolve(null);\n      });\n    });\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### File: `src/services/event-persistence.ts`\n\n```typescript\nimport { appendFile, readFile } from 'fs/promises';\nimport { existsSync } from 'fs';\n\ninterface StoredEvent {\n  id: string;\n  timestamp: string;\n  type: string;\n  data: any;\n  metadata?: {\n    toolName?: string;\n    success?: boolean;\n    durationMs?: number;\n    error?: string;\n  };\n}\n\n/**\n * Simple JSONL-based event store.\n * Hot path: append-only (fast).\n * Cold path: read all for analysis (batch).\n */\nexport class EventPersistence {\n  private filePath: string;\n  private writeBuffer: StoredEvent[] = [];\n  private flushInterval: NodeJS.Timeout | null = null;\n\n  constructor(filePath: string) {\n    this.filePath = filePath;\n    this.startFlushInterval();\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * HOT PATH - called from event emit.\n   * Just buffers in memory, flushes periodically.\n   */\n  store(event: StoredEvent): void {\n    this.writeBuffer.push(event);\n    \n    // Flush if buffer is large\n    if (this.writeBuffer.length >= 10) {\n      this.flush();\n    }\n  }\n\n  private startFlushInterval(): void {\n    this.flushInterval = setInterval(() => {\n      this.flush();\n    }, 2000); // 2 seconds\n  }\n\n  private async flush(): Promise<void> {\n    if (this.writeBuffer.length === 0) return;\n\n    const events = [...this.writeBuffer];\n    this.writeBuffer = [];\n\n    const lines = events.map(e => JSON.stringify(e)).join('\\n') + '\\n';\n    \n    try {\n      await appendFile(this.filePath, lines);\n    } catch (err) {\n      console.error('[EventPersistence] Failed to flush:', err);\n      // Re-add to front of buffer to retry\n      this.writeBuffer = [...events, ...this.writeBuffer];\n    }\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * COLD PATH - called by neuro-memory (via Python).\n   * Reads all events from last N days.\n   */\n  async getRecentEvents(days: number): Promise<StoredEvent[]> {\n    if (!existsSync(this.filePath)) {\n      return [];\n    }\n\n    const content = await readFile(this.filePath, 'utf-8');\n    const lines = content.trim().split('\\n');\n    \n    const cutoff = Date.now() - (days * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000);\n    \n    return lines\n      .map(line => JSON.parse(line) as StoredEvent)\n      .filter(e => new Date(e.timestamp).getTime() > cutoff);\n  }\n\n  stop(): void {\n    if (this.flushInterval) {\n      clearInterval(this.flushInterval);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### File: `/Users/tolga/Desktop/neuro-memory-agent/analyze_for_evolution.py`\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nEntry point for evolution-triggered analysis.\nCalled by NeuroMemoryBridge with event store path.\n\nUsage:\n  python analyze_for_evolution.py --event-store /path/to/events.jsonl --days 30 --focus failures --output json\n\"\"\"\n\nimport argparse\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n# Import your neuro-memory components\nfrom brain import NeuroMemoryAgent, BayesianSurprise, EventSegmenter, TwoStageRetriever\n\ndef load_events_from_store(store_path: str, days_lookback: int) -> list[dict]:\n    \"\"\"Load events from JSONL store (written by TypeScript).\"\"\"\n    events = []\n    cutoff = datetime.now() - timedelta(days=days_lookback)\n    \n    with open(store_path, 'r') as f:\n        for line in f:\n            event = json.loads(line.strip())\n            event_time = datetime.fromisoformat(event['timestamp'])\n            if event_time > cutoff:\n                events.append(event)\n    \n    return events\n\ndef main():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description='Analyze events for evolution')\n    parser.add_argument('--event-store', required=True, help='Path to JSONL event store')\n    parser.add_argument('--days', type=int, default=30, help='Days to look back')\n    parser.add_argument('--focus', choices=['failures', 'all', 'surprises'], default='failures')\n    parser.add_argument('--output', choices=['json', 'text'], default='json')\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n    \n    # 1. Load events\n    events = load_events_from_store(args.event_store, args.days)\n    \n    if len(events) == 0:\n        print(json.dumps({\"error\": \"No events found\"}))\n        sys.exit(0)\n    \n    # 2. Initialize neuro-memory agent\n    agent = NeuroMemoryAgent()\n    \n    # 3. Process events through brain mechanisms\n    for event in events:\n        # Compute Bayesian surprise (detects unexpected events)\n        surprise_score = agent.compute_surprise(event)\n        \n        # Event segmentation (detects task boundaries)\n        is_boundary = agent.detect_boundary(event)\n        \n        # Update memory (online learning)\n        agent.update_memory(event, surprise_score, is_boundary)\n    \n    # 4. Run consolidation (strengthens important patterns, forgets weak ones)\n    agent.consolidate()\n    \n    # 5. Extract insights for evolution\n    if args.focus == 'failures':\n        # Focus on failed tool executions, errors, retries\n        failure_patterns = agent.retrieve_failure_patterns()\n        similar_past = agent.find_similar_failures(current_context=None)\n        \n        insights = {\n            \"failurePatterns\": [\n                {\n                    \"pattern\": p.pattern,\n                    \"frequency\": p.frequency,\n                    \"lastSeen\": p.last_seen.isoformat(),\n                    \"similarPastFailures\": [f.description for f in similar_past if f.pattern_id == p.id]\n                }\n                for p in failure_patterns\n            ],\n            \"surprisingEvents\": agent.get_high_surprise_events(limit=10),\n            \"recommendations\": agent.generate_recommendations(context=\"evolution\"),\n            \"memoryConsolidation\": {\n                \"newPatterns\": agent.stats.new_patterns,\n                \"forgottenPatterns\": agent.stats.forgotten_patterns,\n                \"reinforcedPatterns\": agent.stats.reinforced_patterns\n            }\n        }\n    else:\n        # Full analysis\n        insights = agent.get_full_insights()\n    \n    # 6. Output as JSON (captured by TypeScript bridge)\n    print(json.dumps(insights, indent=2))\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n```\n\n### Integration in Evolution Service\n\n```typescript\n// src/services/evolution.ts\n\nexport class EvolutionService {\n  private neuroMemory: NeuroMemoryBridge;\n  \n  async runEvolution(): Promise<void> {\n    // 1. Get neuro-memory insights (cold path, can take seconds)\n    const insights = await this.neuroMemory.computeInsights({\n      daysLookback: 30,\n      focusArea: 'failures'\n    });\n    \n    // 2. Generate proposals with context\n    const proposals: Proposal[] = [];\n    \n    if (insights) {\n      // Use failure patterns to avoid known bad patterns\n      for (const pattern of insights.failurePatterns) {\n        if (pattern.frequency > 3) {\n          proposals.push({\n            type: 'avoid_pattern',\n            description: `Avoid ${pattern.pattern} - failed ${pattern.frequency} times`,\n            confidence: Math.min(0.9, pattern.frequency * 0.15),\n            evidence: pattern.similarPastFailures\n          });\n        }\n      }\n      \n      // Use recommendations\n      for (const rec of insights.recommendations) {\n        if (rec.confidence > 0.7) {\n          proposals.push({\n            type: rec.type,\n            description: rec.context,\n            confidence: rec.confidence,\n            evidence: ['neuro-memory-analysis']\n          });\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    \n    // 3. Fall back to template-based proposals if no neuro-memory\n    if (proposals.length === 0) {\n      proposals.push(...this.generateTemplateProposals());\n    }\n    \n    // 4. Test and apply proposals...\n    await this.testAndApplyProposals(proposals);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Trade-offs\n\n### ✅ Advantages\n\n| Aspect | Benefit |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Hot path performance** | Zero overhead (no Python, just JSONL append) |\n| **Robustness** | Graceful degradation - evolution works without neuro-memory |\n| **Debuggability** | Single process invocation, easy to inspect |\n| **Batch efficiency** | Bayesian surprise computed on thousands of events at once |\n| **Full history** | Can analyze patterns across weeks, not just last 10 events |\n| **Simplicity** | No message queues, no long-running Python process, no sockets |\n\n### ⚠️ Disadvantages\n\n| Aspect | Mitigation |\n|--------|------------|\n| **Startup latency** | Python VM startup (~500ms) - acceptable for weekly run |\n| **Memory usage** | Loading 30 days of events - keep events small, or use SQLite |\n| **No real-time insights** | Acceptable - evolution doesn't need real-time |\n| **Event store grows** | Add rotation/compaction (keep last 90 days) |\n\n### 🔄 Alternative Considered: Streaming via Unix Socket\n\n```\n[TypeScript] --unix socket--> [Long-running Python process]\n```\n\n**Why I rejected it:**\n- Complex state management (reconnection, buffering)\n- Python process can crash, need supervision\n- Socket overhead on every event (still IPC)\n- Harder to debug\n- Overkill for weekly evolution runs\n\n**Only use this if you need real-time neuro-memory (e.g., for adaptive UI, hot failure detection).**\n\n---\n\n## Performance Analysis\n\n### Hot Path (Event Emit)\n\n| Operation | Time |\n|-----------|------|\n| Create event object | ~0.01ms |\n| Push to buffer | ~0.01ms |\n| **Total per event** | **~0.02ms** |\n| Flush (10 events to disk) | ~2ms (async, non-blocking) |\n\n### Cold Path (Evolution Run - Weekly)\n\n| Operation | Time |\n|-----------|------|\n| Python startup | ~500ms |\n| Load 10K events from JSONL | ~200ms |\n| Compute Bayesian surprise (10K events) | ~2-5s |\n| Event segmentation | ~500ms |\n| Retrieval + consolidation | ~1-2s |\n| **Total** | **~5-10 seconds** |\n\n**Verdict:** Cold path is perfectly acceptable for weekly runs. Hot path has negligible overhead.\n\n---\n\n## Implementation Checklist\n\n1. **Phase 1: Event Persistence (1 day)**\n   - [ ] Implement `EventPersistence` class\n   - [ ] Wire into event emitter (batch queue → event store)\n   - [ ] Add rotation/compaction logic (keep 90 days)\n\n2. **Phase 2: Python Analysis Script (2 days)**\n   - [ ] Create `analyze_for_evolution.py` entry point\n   - [ ] Wire into existing neuro-memory-agent components\n   - [ ] Add CLI args parsing and JSON output\n\n3. **Phase 3: Bridge + Integration (1 day)**\n   - [ ] Implement `NeuroMemoryBridge`\n   - [ ] Wire into `EvolutionService`\n   - [ ] Add fallback logic (template-based proposals)\n\n4. **Phase 4: Testing (1 day)**\n   - [ ] Mock event store, verify Python script output\n   - [ ] Test graceful degradation (Python crash, timeout)\n   - [ ] Benchmark hot path (should be <0.1ms)\n\n---\n\n## Opinionated Recommendations\n\n1. **Start simple.** JSONL is fine. Don't over-engineer with SQLite unless you hit >100K events.\n\n2. **Make neuro-memory optional.** Evolution should work without it. Use environment variable like `NEURO_MEMORY_ENABLED=true`.\n\n3. **Focus on failures first.** Neuro-memory is most valuable for learning from failures (retry patterns, error clusters). Don't try to analyze everything at once.\n\n4. **Log everything.** When neuro-memory runs, log its output. You'll want to debug why it suggested certain proposals.\n\n5. **Version the insights schema.** As neuro-memory evolves, the JSON schema will change. Add a `schemaVersion` field.\n\n---\n\nThis architecture gives you **smarter evolution proposals** with **zero hot-path overhead**. The key insight is that evolution is a cold-path process, so you can afford to compute expensive things (Bayesian surprise, segmentation, consolidation) on-demand rather than continuously."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"zhipu","model":"glm-5","usage":{"input":3043,"output":4733,"cacheRead":11200,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":18976,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1773007564009}}
